WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:03.070
- Hobbs &amp; Shaw is out,
and as of this taping,

00:00:03.070 --> 00:00:06.690
is fresh at 72% with the
critics overall agreeing

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:09.170
that if you know what
you're in for, it delivers.

00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:11.740
Is this another case of so bad it's good?

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:13.540
Or is it actually good?

00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:14.920
Let's find out.

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:16.340
Hi, everyone, I'm Mark Ellis

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:18.530
and welcome back to the After Show.

00:00:18.530 --> 00:00:20.050
This is a spoiler-heavy show,

00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:22.010
so if you've not yet seen Hobbs &amp; Shaw,

00:00:22.010 --> 00:00:23.150
you have been warned.

00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:25.870
And I am thrilled to be
joined by an all-star group

00:00:25.870 --> 00:00:26.990
of panelists here today.

00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:30.280
First up, she is film
correspondent, Nikki Novak.

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:31.830
- Hi!
- I am so excited.

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:32.940
You're making your debut today.

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:34.280
- First time, yes, I'm very excited.

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:36.010
- That's right, and then
also making his debut,

00:00:36.010 --> 00:00:37.270
he's a film critic for The Wrap

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:39.470
and the LA Times, Carlos Aguilar.

00:00:39.470 --> 00:00:40.710
How are you, bud?
- Thank you.

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:43.180
Very excited to talk some
fun scenes about this movie.

00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:45.170
- Yeah, this is a fun movie to talk about.

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:48.270
And our returning champion
via Skype in Atlanta,

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:49.680
you know her from Pay or Wait,

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:51.570
my buddy Sharronda Williams is here.

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:52.820
How are you, Sharronda?

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:55.090
- I am doing just fantabulous today.

00:00:55.090 --> 00:00:56.240
How are you?
- I'm great

00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:58.040
because we get to talk about Hobbs &amp; Shaw,

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:02.000
and it's just a big dumb fun
action movie, in my opinion.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:03.840
So before we actually
get into our first topic,

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:05.480
which we love when y'all
comment and chime in

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:06.980
on each one of our topics,

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:09.940
is this movie fresh or
is this movie rotten?

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:10.830
Carlos, I'm gonna start with you.

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:14.010
Is it fresh or rotten?
- I think it's fresh.

00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:18.160
If you know that the
ridiculous amount of madness

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:21.250
you're in for and if you
appreciate the banter

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:24.070
between these two stars, I
think you'll have a good time.

00:01:24.070 --> 00:01:25.270
I think that's what makes it fresh.

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:28.300
- My vote is fresh as well, so we got 2-0.

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:31.590
Sharronda, are you
contributing to our freshness?

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:34.140
- Of course, this movie
was absolutely ridiculous.

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:36.220
Of course it's gonna be fresh.

00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:38.240
- [Mark] Okay, Nikki, you're the last one.

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:39.410
- I don't know what
you're all talking about

00:01:39.410 --> 00:01:41.625
'cause I think it should be
an award season contender.

00:01:41.625 --> 00:01:42.840
- [Mark] Thank you.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:44.690
- Saying it's a fun popcorn
movie, what're you saying?

00:01:44.690 --> 00:01:45.578
Fresh for me, too.

00:01:45.578 --> 00:01:46.411
Thought it was great.
- It is fresh for me, too,

00:01:46.411 --> 00:01:49.470
because I love when a
movie advertises itself

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:51.380
like hey, this is what
you're getting into.

00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:52.470
- Right.
- So I'm still like

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:53.970
the kid that was mowing everybody's lawns,

00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:55.690
saving up money to go to the movies.

00:01:55.690 --> 00:01:57.660
This movie sold me on what it was,

00:01:57.660 --> 00:01:59.850
and what I got the most out of this movie

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:04.040
was the duo performances of
The Rock and Jason Statham.

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:05.720
I thought they were so much fun together.

00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:06.900
I thought they were comedy gold.

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:07.940
The action was great.

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:09.300
So Nikki, I wanna start with you.

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:11.000
- Yeah.
- Did the Statham-Rock

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:13.460
combination live up to the hype for you?

00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:15.450
- I think it did, and I think, look,

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:16.800
I don't think they would have a spin-off,

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:18.240
even though they have huge star power,

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:20.150
I think they knew what was gonna work.

00:02:20.150 --> 00:02:22.760
And I think we all kind of
knew what was gonna work.

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:25.190
And I mean, I love the two of them,

00:02:25.190 --> 00:02:28.480
but what I really loved was
the addition of Vanessa Kirby.

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:30.180
'Cause I kinda felt like it was a trio

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:31.250
through most of the movie.

00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:33.550
And I thought she really,

00:02:33.550 --> 00:02:35.180
I would never say she outshone them

00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:36.910
because they're both huge
stars in their own right,

00:02:36.910 --> 00:02:39.740
but for me, to sort of
jump into that universe

00:02:39.740 --> 00:02:40.850
and sort of go toe to toe,

00:02:40.850 --> 00:02:42.120
and there was a scene in the beginning

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:43.990
where she and The Rock
are sort of going across

00:02:43.990 --> 00:02:46.710
the table at each other,
and I was just like, damn.

00:02:46.710 --> 00:02:48.440
- [Mark] Yeah, it was fun
watching her have fight scenes

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:49.273
with him, Carlos.
- Yeah.

00:02:49.273 --> 00:02:50.106
- 'Cause I agree with Nikki.

00:02:50.106 --> 00:02:51.580
I think that for the bulk of this movie,

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:54.110
you are following a trio
and it's fun to watch

00:02:54.110 --> 00:02:57.160
each one of them, so it's
maybe not a two-star movie.

00:02:57.160 --> 00:02:59.880
- Right I mean, I think that
the animosity between them

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:01.040
is really exciting.

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:03.380
Especially, you know, I recall
the scene in the airplane.

00:03:03.380 --> 00:03:05.130
So, this kinda of confined space

00:03:05.130 --> 00:03:06.990
in which they can really punch each other,

00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:08.450
but the tension's there.

00:03:08.450 --> 00:03:09.760
And then you add the cameos,

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:12.500
you know, that are kind
of very star heavy,

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:16.583
and it makes for such a
joyful hate-love relationship

00:03:16.583 --> 00:03:18.830
between the two of them.
- Yeah, Sharronda.

00:03:18.830 --> 00:03:20.820
I would agree with the
panelists here on the couch.

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:22.590
Are you as big a fan of The Rock

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:24.963
and Jason Statham's chemistry as we are?

00:03:26.060 --> 00:03:28.540
- Yes, I really love
their chemistry together.

00:03:28.540 --> 00:03:31.210
I still just have certain feelings towards

00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:33.970
Jason Statham's character
'cause justice for Han.

00:03:33.970 --> 00:03:35.984
I just have to say that right there.

00:03:35.984 --> 00:03:37.370
It just, it bothers me still.

00:03:37.370 --> 00:03:39.700
But outside of that, I think
they have great chemistry.

00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:42.130
I could watch an entire two hour movie

00:03:42.130 --> 00:03:44.300
of them just insulting
each other back and forth.

00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:46.330
- And then, how do you
feel about the fight scene

00:03:46.330 --> 00:03:47.820
between The Rock and Vanessa Kirby?

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:49.120
I mean, that was the, because we see her

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:50.610
in the beginning of
the movie and it's fun.

00:03:50.610 --> 00:03:53.380
And then you see her go
toe to toe with The Rock

00:03:53.380 --> 00:03:54.530
and hold her own and I'm like wow,

00:03:54.530 --> 00:03:56.300
she really is delivering.

00:03:56.300 --> 00:03:59.360
She's living up to that
Mission Impossible hype.

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:00.830
- Yes, so I agree with Nikki.

00:04:00.830 --> 00:04:02.700
I really love what Vanessa Kirby brought

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:05.450
to this film and she really held her own.

00:04:05.450 --> 00:04:08.940
And I love how she really
commanded the attention

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:11.310
in the scenes that she's not only fighting

00:04:11.310 --> 00:04:13.580
Dwayne Johnson, but also too,

00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:14.980
just throughout the entire film,

00:04:14.980 --> 00:04:16.860
I just thought she did
a very fantastic job.

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:19.040
- And so then you add Idris
Elba to the mix, Carlos.

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:21.600
And this is a guy who
clearly could've just

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:23.700
mailed in a performance and had
a little bit of fun with it.

00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:25.320
I really got into his character,

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:28.670
and I think I responded to it
more than I thought I would.

00:04:28.670 --> 00:04:31.360
- Right, I mean, he
goes full mad scientist

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:32.300
kind of type of villain, right?

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:34.790
And I love the fact
that we don't really get

00:04:34.790 --> 00:04:37.130
into the specifics of this organization

00:04:37.130 --> 00:04:38.690
and the villains and
the madness of it all.

00:04:38.690 --> 00:04:40.670
It's just him representing the evil

00:04:40.670 --> 00:04:41.503
that's chasing them.

00:04:41.503 --> 00:04:43.120
So I think that he just goes for broke

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:43.953
in this performance.

00:04:43.953 --> 00:04:45.280
- Yeah, because it's a spoiler review,

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:47.160
I can say this, like, Nikki,
at the end of the movie,

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:50.850
when he's off-loaded, I'm
like, we can save him.

00:04:50.850 --> 00:04:53.110
We have time, we can--
- It was emotional.

00:04:53.110 --> 00:04:55.020
- [Mark] Yeah, I was like,
no, let him team up with Hobbs

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:55.880
and Shaw for the next movie.

00:04:55.880 --> 00:04:56.910
- [Nikki] We may have to save this,

00:04:56.910 --> 00:05:00.090
but I see him in the next one
for that very specific reason.

00:05:00.090 --> 00:05:01.981
- [Mark] Oh, we like,
go down and do some--

00:05:01.981 --> 00:05:04.000
- Yeah, yeah.
- Some archeological dig?

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:06.145
- No, I think so, and I think it would be

00:05:06.145 --> 00:05:08.080
a tremendous loss to have this character

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:10.540
just be a one movie kinda
thing because he actually,

00:05:10.540 --> 00:05:13.980
what I loved about it
is in a lot of movies,

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:15.470
superhero movies not so much,

00:05:15.470 --> 00:05:17.450
but in a lot of action movies,

00:05:17.450 --> 00:05:20.110
the villain doesn't necessarily
partake in the action.

00:05:20.110 --> 00:05:22.110
Sometimes, they're just sorta
sitting back giving orders

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:24.010
while all the other guys are doing it.

00:05:24.010 --> 00:05:26.800
But the fact that he was
this cyber, you know,

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:28.760
Terminator-type person.

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:30.180
And I've seen him in a lot of movies,

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:33.790
but I've never felt the
presence that I felt from him

00:05:33.790 --> 00:05:35.480
in this movie.
- Yeah, Carlos,

00:05:35.480 --> 00:05:36.700
do you agree with that take?

00:05:36.700 --> 00:05:38.527
- Yeah, I mean, also he has
this whole vendetta, you know?

00:05:38.527 --> 00:05:40.700
There's like the emotional component

00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:43.930
that he's a villain that
has this master plan behind,

00:05:43.930 --> 00:05:46.640
but he also hates Jason
Statham's character,

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:48.480
so he goes for him.

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:49.410
- Sharronda, you on the same page?

00:05:49.410 --> 00:05:53.570
You wanna see more of our
biogenetically engineered

00:05:53.570 --> 00:05:56.380
super villain, Idris Elba?
- Are you kidding me?

00:05:56.380 --> 00:05:57.230
Of course I do.

00:05:57.230 --> 00:05:58.590
He had so much swag.

00:05:58.590 --> 00:05:59.950
The entire film, I was just like,

00:05:59.950 --> 00:06:02.040
I know that he is on the bad side,

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:04.500
but if he is, I don't
wanna be right anymore.

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:05.790
He plays a great villain,

00:06:05.790 --> 00:06:08.570
even for when I was first
introduced to him in The Wire,

00:06:08.570 --> 00:06:11.687
he just always plays a
very charismatic villain.

00:06:11.687 --> 00:06:13.100
- All right, so we're having a good time

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:14.210
talking about the movie so far

00:06:14.210 --> 00:06:15.380
and we all like the performances.

00:06:15.380 --> 00:06:18.390
Here comes the first real
curve ball of the show.

00:06:18.390 --> 00:06:19.640
Nikki, I'm gonna start with you.

00:06:19.640 --> 00:06:22.860
Does this movie prove, in
sort of an inverse way,

00:06:22.860 --> 00:06:24.560
that The Rock is actually better

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:27.860
when he has a costar to play off of?

00:06:27.860 --> 00:06:29.120
Because his individual movies,

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:31.110
if you take something like a Skyscraper

00:06:31.110 --> 00:06:32.700
or something like San Andreas, Rampage,

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:34.470
it's not quite as high on the Tomato meter

00:06:34.470 --> 00:06:36.890
as a Central Intelligence
or some of these other ones

00:06:36.890 --> 00:06:38.530
he gets to team up with, like Jumanji.

00:06:38.530 --> 00:06:40.580
So do you think that this
is proof that The Rock

00:06:40.580 --> 00:06:42.480
needs a costar to have a great film?

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:43.870
- I don't, and I'll tell you why.

00:06:43.870 --> 00:06:45.880
I actually thought San
Andreas really worked.

00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:48.070
And I think it did really
well at the box office,

00:06:48.070 --> 00:06:49.900
and I think it was one of those movies,

00:06:49.900 --> 00:06:51.600
one of those so bad, it's good movies,

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:53.610
but where it really was an enjoyable movie

00:06:53.610 --> 00:06:54.880
at the end of the day.

00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:56.130
You know, Baywatch, not so much,

00:06:56.130 --> 00:06:57.240
but that was an ensemble.

00:06:57.240 --> 00:06:59.060
So you could argue that was an ensemble.

00:06:59.060 --> 00:07:02.260
Zac Efron, those two
together had great chemistry.

00:07:02.260 --> 00:07:04.040
I think it's more the storyline

00:07:04.040 --> 00:07:05.930
and the few misses that he's had,

00:07:05.930 --> 00:07:08.450
unfortunately kind of have been in a row.

00:07:08.450 --> 00:07:10.710
So you feel it more than
if there had been a hit

00:07:10.710 --> 00:07:11.670
and then a miss and then a hit,

00:07:11.670 --> 00:07:13.360
but then he did have Jumanji.

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:14.530
But no, I think, look.

00:07:14.530 --> 00:07:15.930
I mean, you look at his following

00:07:15.930 --> 00:07:17.690
and what he's really smart about doing

00:07:17.690 --> 00:07:19.630
is building his international following.

00:07:19.630 --> 00:07:21.720
And I think what he's doing right now

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:23.973
is delivering what he thinks fans want,

00:07:24.963 --> 00:07:26.340
and he's said that before.

00:07:26.340 --> 00:07:28.260
So I think if he keeps
going down that route

00:07:28.260 --> 00:07:30.880
of I'm gonna do these action-type films,

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:31.713
you're gonna have some misses,

00:07:31.713 --> 00:07:35.110
especially when you're in
an era where it's so hard

00:07:35.110 --> 00:07:36.760
if you're not in a universe,

00:07:36.760 --> 00:07:38.980
to establish a one-off.
- Well, sure.

00:07:38.980 --> 00:07:42.060
I mean, if I put you in the lineage

00:07:42.060 --> 00:07:44.170
of Arnold Schwarzenegger
movies or Stalone movies,

00:07:44.170 --> 00:07:45.280
there's a lot of misses,

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:47.680
maybe arguably more misses than hits.

00:07:47.680 --> 00:07:49.940
Sharronda, if I drop you
off in a movie theater

00:07:49.940 --> 00:07:51.390
and I say, you can go see The Rock

00:07:51.390 --> 00:07:52.600
headline a movie by himself

00:07:52.600 --> 00:07:55.480
or you get to go see him
costar with one of his

00:07:55.480 --> 00:07:57.270
either action or comedy buddies,

00:07:57.270 --> 00:07:59.480
which theater are you going into?

00:07:59.480 --> 00:08:02.810
- I mean, personally, I'm going
to go into the comedy door.

00:08:02.810 --> 00:08:04.910
However, I think it's not fair to say that

00:08:04.910 --> 00:08:08.010
he can't command a
movie with just himself.

00:08:08.010 --> 00:08:10.090
I do think that it's more of a

00:08:10.090 --> 00:08:12.300
understanding what you're
getting yourself into.

00:08:12.300 --> 00:08:15.050
I think as an audience,
we tend to take movies

00:08:15.050 --> 00:08:17.520
starring himself more seriously

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:20.580
than if we hear Hobbs &amp; Shaw
or Central Intelligence,

00:08:20.580 --> 00:08:23.910
I think we expect to have
this fun enjoyable ride.

00:08:23.910 --> 00:08:25.180
- Yeah, Carlos, you on the same page?

00:08:25.180 --> 00:08:26.930
Would you rather see him team up

00:08:26.930 --> 00:08:28.960
in more of a comedic aspect?

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:31.660
- I mean, I think that
definitely works in this one.

00:08:31.660 --> 00:08:33.110
The banter between them is really fun,

00:08:33.110 --> 00:08:34.440
but he's a superstar.

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:36.160
I feel like there's no doubt that

00:08:36.160 --> 00:08:38.570
when you see his name on that
billboard, you wanna see it,

00:08:38.570 --> 00:08:39.740
even if it's not Hobbs &amp; Shaw,

00:08:39.740 --> 00:08:41.580
even if it's not Rampage or whatnot.

00:08:41.580 --> 00:08:44.220
I feel like he commands the
attention of the audience.

00:08:44.220 --> 00:08:46.700
And you know, I was at the
premiere for this movie

00:08:46.700 --> 00:08:48.850
and there was a sort of like a moment

00:08:48.850 --> 00:08:50.920
in which there was an incident with fire

00:08:50.920 --> 00:08:52.330
and they had to stop the movie,

00:08:52.330 --> 00:08:55.470
and he came out and kinda like
came to calm the audience.

00:08:55.470 --> 00:08:56.830
- Was this real fire
or was this like hey--

00:08:56.830 --> 00:08:58.503
- It was real fire.
- It was real.

00:08:58.503 --> 00:08:59.337
I was there, too.

00:08:59.337 --> 00:09:00.170
It was an interesting moment.

00:09:00.170 --> 00:09:01.050
- It was real fire.
- Wow.

00:09:01.050 --> 00:09:02.710
- And he came out and like
kind of like, you know,

00:09:02.710 --> 00:09:04.910
commanded the audience
and you know, made it fun

00:09:04.910 --> 00:09:06.960
and kind of brought everyone back to like,

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:09.580
an enjoyable, after the
stressful kind of moment,

00:09:09.580 --> 00:09:10.413
he brought it back.

00:09:10.413 --> 00:09:12.720
So I think he's just, people love him,

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:14.078
and he could command it by himself.

00:09:14.078 --> 00:09:14.930
- He almost made it better.

00:09:14.930 --> 00:09:16.953
I almost thought that they
should've video'd that

00:09:16.953 --> 00:09:19.310
and just had an intermission
for everybody watching it

00:09:19.310 --> 00:09:22.000
across the board and just
insert that 'cause he did.

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:24.170
He upped the ante and
then he sort of like,

00:09:24.170 --> 00:09:25.460
nobody else, I think, could do that.

00:09:25.460 --> 00:09:26.780
- Yeah, I mean, the guy conquered

00:09:26.780 --> 00:09:28.120
the San Andreas Fault earthquake.

00:09:28.120 --> 00:09:30.490
I think he's pretty good with
a fire in a movie theater.

00:09:30.490 --> 00:09:33.730
So The Rock and Jason Statham
and then Vanessa Kirby as well

00:09:33.730 --> 00:09:35.080
are your headliners of the movie,

00:09:35.080 --> 00:09:36.810
but I think a lot of people, Carlos,

00:09:36.810 --> 00:09:39.550
are responding to the cameos that we had.

00:09:39.550 --> 00:09:41.650
We had Ryan Reynolds.

00:09:41.650 --> 00:09:45.990
Kevin Hart, I thought, stole
that entire airplane scene.

00:09:45.990 --> 00:09:47.100
Did you enjoy the cameos?

00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:48.480
Do you think they took
away from the story?

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:50.110
Where are you with that?
- Oh, I love them.

00:09:50.110 --> 00:09:52.610
I definitely prefer the
Ryan Reynolds character.

00:09:52.610 --> 00:09:54.070
I thought he was incredibly funny.

00:09:54.070 --> 00:09:56.040
I like to think that
that's him in real life

00:09:56.040 --> 00:09:58.330
really wanting to be The
Rock's best friend, you know?

00:09:58.330 --> 00:10:02.530
'Cause it feels just so
earnest and fun and you know,

00:10:02.530 --> 00:10:05.030
wild in his kind of antics
to become his friend

00:10:05.030 --> 00:10:07.850
that The Rock being such a superstar,

00:10:07.850 --> 00:10:09.730
I feel like there may
be many actors out there

00:10:09.730 --> 00:10:12.240
that wanna be his best friend
so I thought it was fun.

00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:13.600
- Sharronda, I'm watching that scene play

00:10:13.600 --> 00:10:15.490
and I'm thinking, this
is exactly what The Rock

00:10:15.490 --> 00:10:19.350
and Kevin Hart's social
media feud is like.

00:10:19.350 --> 00:10:21.020
And we got to actually watch that factor

00:10:21.020 --> 00:10:23.370
into the storyline a
little bit so, I loved it.

00:10:24.830 --> 00:10:28.020
- The airplane scene, I
literally yelled out loud.

00:10:28.020 --> 00:10:30.290
I screamed when I say
him in the first place.

00:10:30.290 --> 00:10:32.350
I love that entire sequence.

00:10:32.350 --> 00:10:33.880
And even with Ryan Reynolds.

00:10:33.880 --> 00:10:37.330
And what I will say is after
the first set of cameos,

00:10:37.330 --> 00:10:39.700
when the second round
came in, I was just like,

00:10:39.700 --> 00:10:40.760
mmm, I'm kinda over it.

00:10:40.760 --> 00:10:42.830
I was happy with the first one.

00:10:42.830 --> 00:10:45.140
I don't know if we needed
to go into the second one.

00:10:45.140 --> 00:10:46.740
It made me feel more as though

00:10:46.740 --> 00:10:48.530
they're gonna introduce the characters

00:10:48.530 --> 00:10:50.920
into this Hobbs &amp; Shaw franchise.

00:10:50.920 --> 00:10:53.160
- Yeah, so, I'll give Rob
Delaney a shout out, too,

00:10:53.160 --> 00:10:55.300
because he also makes a fun cameo.

00:10:55.300 --> 00:10:58.270
Nikki, which of the cameos do
you think enhanced the film?

00:10:58.270 --> 00:10:59.600
- Kevin's was my favorite.

00:10:59.600 --> 00:11:00.760
I always love Ryan Reynolds,

00:11:00.760 --> 00:11:02.580
but I felt like Ryan Reynolds
was doing Ryan Reynolds.

00:11:02.580 --> 00:11:05.370
Not that Kevin wasn't, but I felt like

00:11:05.370 --> 00:11:07.870
there was a couple moments that Ryan's,

00:11:07.870 --> 00:11:09.100
there was a few lines that I was like,

00:11:09.100 --> 00:11:12.657
oh, Ryan's like really like,
pushing almost a little bit.

00:11:12.657 --> 00:11:14.880
- Him and David Leitch have
that great Deadpool rapport, so.

00:11:14.880 --> 00:11:16.460
- Yeah, yeah, I felt that.

00:11:16.460 --> 00:11:18.320
But I think that Kevin
Hart was such a surprise.

00:11:18.320 --> 00:11:20.630
And I've now seen this movie three times.

00:11:20.630 --> 00:11:22.560
And I saw it the first time by myself,

00:11:22.560 --> 00:11:23.620
the second time at the premiere,

00:11:23.620 --> 00:11:26.570
and the third time with a lot
of Millennial influencers,

00:11:26.570 --> 00:11:29.530
so it was three totally
different responses.

00:11:29.530 --> 00:11:30.490
But I will say in the premiere,

00:11:30.490 --> 00:11:31.530
I'm sure you felt the same thing,

00:11:31.530 --> 00:11:34.920
when Kevin's face first
appeared on screen,

00:11:34.920 --> 00:11:38.100
the place erupted, totally erupted.

00:11:38.100 --> 00:11:40.580
With Ryan, I think 'cause it
was towards the beginning,

00:11:40.580 --> 00:11:42.900
it was more like, oh wow,
it was more surprise.

00:11:42.900 --> 00:11:44.690
But I think Kevin really.

00:11:44.690 --> 00:11:46.900
I actually wanted to see
one more scene with Kevin

00:11:46.900 --> 00:11:47.733
in the end.

00:11:47.733 --> 00:11:49.610
I wanted him to come in and
save the day in the end.

00:11:49.610 --> 00:11:51.530
I wanted him to be the sort of person that

00:11:51.530 --> 00:11:53.200
came through with a key thing in the end

00:11:53.200 --> 00:11:54.171
that sort of helped them win.

00:11:54.171 --> 00:11:55.360
- He could've flown a
helicopter or something

00:11:55.360 --> 00:11:57.120
into that last scene--
- Yeah, something.

00:11:57.120 --> 00:11:58.030
- In Samoa.

00:11:58.030 --> 00:12:00.630
There's one other sort
of maybe kind of cameo

00:12:00.630 --> 00:12:02.220
that's at least rumored
that we'll talk about

00:12:02.220 --> 00:12:03.520
later on in the show, but right now,

00:12:03.520 --> 00:12:05.670
I wanna get to the meat of Hobbs &amp; Shaw,

00:12:05.670 --> 00:12:08.330
which is those incredible
action set pieces.

00:12:08.330 --> 00:12:10.620
Like I said, David Leitch is
the director here, Carlos,

00:12:10.620 --> 00:12:12.930
and you know he knows how to
pull off action very well,

00:12:12.930 --> 00:12:14.820
both close hand-to-hand combat

00:12:14.820 --> 00:12:17.480
then also like these big, giant, you know,

00:12:17.480 --> 00:12:19.530
it's just getting crazier
and crazier every movie.

00:12:19.530 --> 00:12:23.370
So, did the action live
up to the hype for you?

00:12:23.370 --> 00:12:24.380
- Of course, yeah.

00:12:24.380 --> 00:12:26.360
The scenes are so ridiculous.

00:12:26.360 --> 00:12:29.090
You have to commit to
this disbelief, right,

00:12:29.090 --> 00:12:31.310
that nothing in this world
functions like reality.

00:12:31.310 --> 00:12:33.460
They're almost like
superheros at this point,

00:12:33.460 --> 00:12:34.530
the kind of stunts they pull.

00:12:34.530 --> 00:12:37.720
So that final sequence with
the trucks and the helicopter

00:12:37.720 --> 00:12:40.080
in Samoa is just so off the chain that

00:12:41.100 --> 00:12:43.150
watching it in a big screen
with a crowd of people

00:12:43.150 --> 00:12:47.420
is just such an emotional
experience in a way that

00:12:47.420 --> 00:12:48.980
watching it alone, it
probably wouldn't work,

00:12:48.980 --> 00:12:51.050
but on a theater with that level of action

00:12:51.050 --> 00:12:52.040
is just so much fun.

00:12:52.040 --> 00:12:53.740
- [Mark] That poor
helicopter, Nikki, at the end.

00:12:53.740 --> 00:12:55.270
I mean, that thing goes through so much.

00:12:55.270 --> 00:12:56.680
- [Nikki] By the way, that wasn't CGI.

00:12:56.680 --> 00:12:58.657
The Rock actually did that.
(laughing)

00:12:58.657 --> 00:13:00.210
- He actually did all that stuff.

00:13:00.210 --> 00:13:02.350
He hired the best towers in the business

00:13:02.350 --> 00:13:04.590
to get their chains exactly
connected to the truck.

00:13:04.590 --> 00:13:06.060
Do you think that this was the best action

00:13:06.060 --> 00:13:08.930
that we've seen in a Fast &amp; Furious

00:13:08.930 --> 00:13:09.784
or Fast &amp; Furious Presents movie?

00:13:09.784 --> 00:13:12.130
- No, I wouldn't say it was the best.

00:13:12.130 --> 00:13:14.500
See, for me, it was sort of a two parter.

00:13:14.500 --> 00:13:16.500
Some of it felt like the sorta close up,

00:13:16.500 --> 00:13:18.170
the slow mos felt very David Leitch.

00:13:18.170 --> 00:13:20.250
It felt very Atomic Blonde, or you know,

00:13:20.250 --> 00:13:21.380
what he's done in the past.

00:13:21.380 --> 00:13:23.440
And then this sort of
big action set pieces

00:13:23.440 --> 00:13:26.310
like the helicopter and that
felt very Fast &amp; Furiuos to me.

00:13:26.310 --> 00:13:28.180
So they felt like two different things

00:13:28.180 --> 00:13:29.890
and very two different elements.

00:13:29.890 --> 00:13:31.380
In terms of like the big action,

00:13:31.380 --> 00:13:33.900
if it wasn't for the last
helicopter bit and The Rock,

00:13:33.900 --> 00:13:35.710
'cause that's what I love
about Fast &amp; Furious.

00:13:35.710 --> 00:13:37.383
I want to suspend my disbelief,

00:13:38.570 --> 00:13:40.010
and I want to enjoy those moments

00:13:40.010 --> 00:13:42.106
that you could never, it's
almost superhero-esque.

00:13:42.106 --> 00:13:43.740
- It really is.
- In a way, in a way.

00:13:43.740 --> 00:13:47.460
But I felt like David Lietch
did what he does really well

00:13:47.460 --> 00:13:48.820
and I love those sort of close up,

00:13:48.820 --> 00:13:51.110
and I love the two three-way
fights that we've had

00:13:51.110 --> 00:13:52.920
'cause I don't think we've
seen that before in cinema.

00:13:52.920 --> 00:13:55.930
And I think Idris really
pulled that off as well.

00:13:55.930 --> 00:13:57.300
- So you said something very interesting,

00:13:57.300 --> 00:13:58.530
and Sharronda, I wanna
get your take on this.

00:13:58.530 --> 00:14:01.330
So, it's kind of like
these blockbuster movies

00:14:01.330 --> 00:14:03.490
are becoming more and
more superhero films,

00:14:03.490 --> 00:14:05.570
so it's almost like you
can incorporate them

00:14:05.570 --> 00:14:06.939
in the MCU seamlessly.

00:14:06.939 --> 00:14:07.772
- Right.
- Right.

00:14:07.772 --> 00:14:09.390
- Sharronda, do you like this trend

00:14:09.390 --> 00:14:11.780
that it's becoming more
like a Marvel movie

00:14:11.780 --> 00:14:14.290
every time you go see one
of these blockbusters,

00:14:14.290 --> 00:14:15.810
particularly a Fast &amp; Furious movie,

00:14:15.810 --> 00:14:18.540
where they were into street racers.

00:14:18.540 --> 00:14:20.520
Back when I was a kid, they
were just living their lives

00:14:20.520 --> 00:14:22.970
a quarter mile at a time and
souping up some Honda Civics.

00:14:22.970 --> 00:14:25.710
And now, they're literally
stopping genetically engineered

00:14:25.710 --> 00:14:29.010
viruses that are perpetrated
by super bad guys.

00:14:29.010 --> 00:14:30.600
Are you a fan of this trend

00:14:30.600 --> 00:14:32.544
with the Fast &amp; Furious films?

00:14:32.544 --> 00:14:34.330
- I definitely am.

00:14:34.330 --> 00:14:36.160
I mean, there's been so many movies.

00:14:36.160 --> 00:14:37.370
You have to start evolving,

00:14:37.370 --> 00:14:39.470
you have to start introducing these new

00:14:39.470 --> 00:14:42.530
even more ridiculous and
crazy concepts into the film.

00:14:42.530 --> 00:14:45.210
I mean, The Rock literally
jumps out of a building

00:14:45.210 --> 00:14:47.240
and like, scales a building down a rope.

00:14:47.240 --> 00:14:48.330
Like you should not have a hand,

00:14:48.330 --> 00:14:49.400
you shouldn't have an arm.

00:14:49.400 --> 00:14:51.310
But it was cool watching him do it.

00:14:51.310 --> 00:14:52.880
I love the action in it.

00:14:52.880 --> 00:14:55.550
I don't think that it fully
lives up to the absurdity

00:14:55.550 --> 00:14:57.630
of the Fast &amp; Furious movies.

00:14:57.630 --> 00:14:59.630
I think I've seen better, more crazier,

00:14:59.630 --> 00:15:01.440
unrealistic things happen,

00:15:01.440 --> 00:15:03.180
but I had a great time watching it.

00:15:03.180 --> 00:15:04.770
- Yeah, I don't think we're gonna see

00:15:06.376 --> 00:15:10.230
the cars going out of the
airplane again any time soon.

00:15:10.230 --> 00:15:12.360
I would not parachute with a car.

00:15:12.360 --> 00:15:15.200
Friendly, helpful tip
for all you kids at home.

00:15:15.200 --> 00:15:18.120
Carlos, your favorite
action scene in this movie.

00:15:18.120 --> 00:15:20.460
Which one did you dig the best?

00:15:20.460 --> 00:15:22.450
- I think it's that one by far.

00:15:22.450 --> 00:15:24.440
And when I talk about the one in Samoa,

00:15:24.440 --> 00:15:25.930
I mean the beginning
with the kind of like,

00:15:25.930 --> 00:15:27.740
combat with the old school weapons.

00:15:27.740 --> 00:15:28.840
- I love that.
- I think that's

00:15:28.840 --> 00:15:30.110
very interesting and sort of like

00:15:30.110 --> 00:15:33.480
brings it back down to,
you know, not using guns

00:15:33.480 --> 00:15:35.520
and kind of like the man on man.

00:15:35.520 --> 00:15:36.740
So, the beginning of that.

00:15:36.740 --> 00:15:38.950
And then going into the
trucks and the airplane

00:15:38.950 --> 00:15:40.900
and you know, sort of like
this big finale, I think,

00:15:40.900 --> 00:15:43.530
just brings it home because
it has a cultural element

00:15:43.530 --> 00:15:46.110
as well, which I think
elevates the franchise.

00:15:46.110 --> 00:15:48.410
- Yeah, and I like the
final little fist fight

00:15:48.410 --> 00:15:51.120
that we had at the waterfall
just because it was,

00:15:51.120 --> 00:15:52.570
you know, it was a little on the nose,

00:15:52.570 --> 00:15:53.690
but it's like hey, Hobbs and Shaw,

00:15:53.690 --> 00:15:56.010
they finally realized
if they work together,

00:15:56.010 --> 00:15:57.710
they're gonna be able to,
and that's the only way

00:15:57.710 --> 00:15:59.390
they're gonna be able
to defeat this villain.

00:15:59.390 --> 00:16:00.940
The only real complaint I've heard

00:16:00.940 --> 00:16:02.620
about any of the action is that

00:16:02.620 --> 00:16:05.220
it started to drag the
film down a little bit.

00:16:05.220 --> 00:16:07.260
Does anybody feel that way
that maybe we could've used

00:16:07.260 --> 00:16:08.287
a little less action?

00:16:08.287 --> 00:16:11.370
- I actually felt like, I
know the movie's very long

00:16:11.370 --> 00:16:12.620
and that's a critique that I've heard,

00:16:12.620 --> 00:16:13.640
that it's two hours and 15 minutes.

00:16:13.640 --> 00:16:14.473
It could've been shorter.

00:16:14.473 --> 00:16:16.010
There wasn't anything I disliked about it.

00:16:16.010 --> 00:16:17.980
There wasn't anything
that, other than I think

00:16:17.980 --> 00:16:19.700
that sort of opening sequence

00:16:19.700 --> 00:16:21.500
before you see them side
by side in the morning

00:16:21.500 --> 00:16:23.200
getting their coffee and
their very different ways

00:16:23.200 --> 00:16:24.570
of getting ready for work,

00:16:24.570 --> 00:16:27.440
there was a sequence where
they both go after like,

00:16:27.440 --> 00:16:29.270
where's the virus and
they fight a bad guy,

00:16:29.270 --> 00:16:31.940
and you know, The Rock tattoos
the thing on the guy's head.

00:16:31.940 --> 00:16:34.130
And I felt like you didn't need that.

00:16:34.130 --> 00:16:35.840
I felt that whole
sequence you didn't need.

00:16:35.840 --> 00:16:36.990
I think you could've set it up,

00:16:36.990 --> 00:16:39.820
almost started the film
off with the side by side

00:16:39.820 --> 00:16:40.790
of them getting ready.

00:16:40.790 --> 00:16:42.620
I don't think you needed that whole bit

00:16:42.620 --> 00:16:44.830
because it didn't really
add anything to the film.

00:16:44.830 --> 00:16:47.130
And I feel like the two big

00:16:47.130 --> 00:16:48.850
second and third act
set pieces were enough.

00:16:48.850 --> 00:16:49.977
- Sharronda, you agree with that?

00:16:49.977 --> 00:16:53.270
You think we could've
lost that little breakfast

00:16:53.270 --> 00:16:55.340
slash gym montage at the beginning

00:16:55.340 --> 00:16:58.780
or what part of the film felt
the most out of place to you?

00:16:58.780 --> 00:17:00.750
- I like the intro 'cause
it just establishes

00:17:00.750 --> 00:17:02.690
how different they are.

00:17:02.690 --> 00:17:04.730
But for me, while watching the film,

00:17:04.730 --> 00:17:07.713
I literally thought the movie
ended three different times.

00:17:08.760 --> 00:17:10.040
I was like okay, it's about to end.

00:17:10.040 --> 00:17:11.270
Lemme get my stuff together.

00:17:11.270 --> 00:17:14.280
Then I was like no, there's
like a whole other set piece

00:17:14.280 --> 00:17:16.180
that we have to go through.

00:17:16.180 --> 00:17:18.620
So for those moments, it did drag for me.

00:17:18.620 --> 00:17:22.320
It wasn't necessarily
it had too much action.

00:17:22.320 --> 00:17:24.650
It was just a little bit too much going on

00:17:24.650 --> 00:17:26.350
from like a storyline.

00:17:26.350 --> 00:17:28.730
It felt as though they were
trying to check all of the beats

00:17:28.730 --> 00:17:30.780
of what a Fast &amp; Furious movie is.

00:17:30.780 --> 00:17:33.230
We have to have family,
we have to have conflict.

00:17:33.230 --> 00:17:35.920
And just, I just think
that they had a little bit

00:17:35.920 --> 00:17:38.360
too many ideas with Hobbs &amp; Shaw

00:17:38.360 --> 00:17:41.110
that could've been left
on the cutting room floor.

00:17:41.110 --> 00:17:42.380
- Carlos, do you think we could've trimmed

00:17:42.380 --> 00:17:44.510
some of the fat off this movie?

00:17:44.510 --> 00:17:46.100
- Maybe a little bit.

00:17:46.100 --> 00:17:48.520
I go back to the scene, you
know, the Mexican actress,

00:17:48.520 --> 00:17:51.280
Eiza Gonzales, is in the
film for like, one second.

00:17:51.280 --> 00:17:53.960
And I feel like, either
they could've chosen to

00:17:53.960 --> 00:17:56.790
use her character more
or cut it out entirely

00:17:56.790 --> 00:17:59.580
'cause it just feels like
she's just introduced briefly,

00:17:59.580 --> 00:18:01.410
very quickly, even though
she's in the poster

00:18:01.410 --> 00:18:03.613
and she's kinda like, you know,

00:18:03.613 --> 00:18:05.700
being held as part of the movie,

00:18:05.700 --> 00:18:08.740
she's very, you know, for
a brief moment in the film.

00:18:08.740 --> 00:18:10.700
So either, for me, it was either cut it

00:18:10.700 --> 00:18:12.590
or expand that character.
- That's a great point.

00:18:12.590 --> 00:18:14.260
She was like the Captain
Phasma of this movie

00:18:14.260 --> 00:18:16.030
where it's like, you're
on a lot of the artwork

00:18:16.030 --> 00:18:18.300
and then it's like hey,
you showed up and goodbye.

00:18:18.300 --> 00:18:20.760
I personally like the breakfast montage

00:18:20.760 --> 00:18:22.610
just because that's two
different ways of waking up

00:18:22.610 --> 00:18:23.563
than I'm used to.

00:18:23.563 --> 00:18:25.100
- No, that's the part I wish they kept in.

00:18:25.100 --> 00:18:26.811
I wish that was the
opening scene of the movie,

00:18:26.811 --> 00:18:28.690
and cut out the action-y part, yeah.

00:18:28.690 --> 00:18:30.780
- Yeah, I'm not doing what The Rock does.

00:18:30.780 --> 00:18:32.380
I'm not waking up at
six a.m., first of all.

00:18:32.380 --> 00:18:34.220
Like mine would be like 10:30 a.m.

00:18:34.220 --> 00:18:35.370
And I'm not going to the gym,

00:18:35.370 --> 00:18:36.740
